Chapter: CapGpuMemDenied

 Runtime probe for the GPU memory window guard: poke-32 and peek-32
 consult the per-process capability word before touching the window
 at 0xBE000000. The program declares Gpu.Memory, so the boot grant
 derived from its own manifest carries bit 18 and the first write
 lands. It then strips its own grant word in the process table and
 writes again, and the kernel refuses with -1.

 The last two lines are the ones that matter. A denial that returned
 -1 while still performing the write would look identical from the
 caller's seat, so the probe restores the bit and reads the word back:
 it still holds the value written under the grant, which is the only
 evidence that the refused write never reached the window.

 The capability word lives at 20536 -- proc-table-base 20480, entry 0,
 cap offset 56 -- which is far below the window, so the pokes that
 strip and restore the grant run the guard's check-free path. That is
 what makes them usable to disarm the guard being tested.

Section: Body

  proc0-cap-addr : Integer = 20536

  gpu-cmd : Integer = #BE000000

  cap-gpu-memory-bit : Integer = 262144

  strip-grant : Integer -> Integer
  strip-grant (x) =
    let lo = poke-32 proc0-cap-addr 0 0
    in let hi = poke-32 proc0-cap-addr 4 0
    in 0

  restore-grant : Integer -> Integer
  restore-grant (x) = poke-32 proc0-cap-addr 0 cap-gpu-memory-bit

  opening : [Console, Gpu.Memory] Nothing = act
    granted <- gpu-mem-write gpu-cmd 0 12345
    print-line-uni ("granted write: " & show granted)
    readback <- gpu-mem-read gpu-cmd 0
    print-line-uni ("granted read: " & show readback)
    print-line-uni ("stripped: " & show (strip-grant 0))
    denied <- gpu-mem-write gpu-cmd 0 999
    print-line-uni ("denied write: " & show denied)
    print-line-uni ("restored: " & show (restore-grant 0))
    after <- gpu-mem-read gpu-cmd 0
    print-line-uni ("after restore: " & show after)
  end

